# Nissan Sunny N14 five-door hatchback, 1991-1995.
#
# The last Sunny sold in Europe: the rounded N14 body with the small grille
# badge and the SUNNY lettering across the tailgate. Sold in Britain from
# March 1991. The three-door hatchback, the four-door saloon and the estate
# were also UK bodies and are not represented; the five-door is modelled
# because it is the body with a true level side photograph.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nissan_Pulsar
#
# Dimensions 4.146 x 1.660 x 1.400 m, wheelbase 2.430 m (Wikipedia infobox;
# the saloon is 4.230 m).
#
# Shape reference: image 1 is a true level side elevation of an Australian
# Pulsar-badged N14 five-door, the same shell as the UK Sunny. Images 2 and 3
# are a UK three-door, used for the nose, the tail, the badges and the lamp
# forms; image 4 is a saloon front, used for the grille and headlamps.
#
# Colours: grade (d); no UK colour chart was found. White, Red, Blue and Silver
# are plain names. Weights 12, 10, 9, 8, total 39, provisional.
#
# Speed 175 km/h. Comfort 70/285, on the line 180 + 1.5 x 70. Classes 1,2.
# Dates: intro March 1991; retire staggered 2010-2013 as a road tail, the last
# Sunnys being replaced by the Almera late in 1995.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/nissan-sunny-n14.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/nissan-sunny-n14.dae. References:
# citycars/references/nissan-sunny-n14/ (gitignored; Wikimedia Commons).
#
# Accepted deviations: the five-door hatchback only; no three-door or saloon
# body geometry was copied. The bumper corner transitions and the lamp wrap
# are faceted, and the wheel covers and badging are simplified.


obj=citycar
name=NissanSunnyN14(White)
copyright=JamesPetts

DistributionWeight=12
intro_year=1991
intro_month=3
retire_year=2013
